**Test filters.** For this step these gates run only the tests their filter selects (plus any inherited expected-red tests); put the tests this step adds or changes where a filter value finds them:
- `constructor-unit-tests`: 

**How to implement.** What logic receives: the opened kit (001 §P 'Logic receives a KitContext'; the S4.1 brief: KitContext holds the proxy, the loaded KitFile, the access and the clock, and a read-only KitContext writes nothing at all, not even .oc/; .spec-md §3.12). In `KitFolders.fs`, after `KitProxy.createInMemory`, each doc one to three lines citing spec 0050 S4.1: (1) `KitError` (step 122, in `Kit.fs`) gains `KitReadOnlyErr of reason : string` (a write asked of a kit opened read-only, the reason naming the verb), rendered `the kit is open read-only: <reason>`. (2) `type KitProxy with member this.readOnlyView : KitProxy` answers the same proxy with every write refused: `initialiseKit`, `writeKitFile`, `writeKindFile`, `deleteKindFile` and `acquireLock` answer `KitReadOnlyErr` naming the field and write nothing; `releaseLock` answers `Ok ()` and does nothing; `readKitFile`, `listKindFiles`, `readKindFile`, `readDataFile`, `recordStamps` and `changedKitFiles` pass through, because they write nothing. A field a later step adds that writes is refused here too. (3) `KitContext = { proxy : KitProxy; kitFile : KitFile; access : KitAccess; clock : ClockProvider }`. `static member openKit (proxy : KitProxy) (access : KitAccess) (clock : ClockProvider) : Result<KitContext, KitError>` opens the kit in this order. Under `ReadOnlyKitAccess` the context's proxy is `proxy.readOnlyView`; under `ReadWriteKitAccess` it is `proxy`. It reads the kit file (an error passes through and nothing is written). Under `ReadWriteKitAccess` it acquires the lock at `clock.now ()`; `KitLockedErr` passes through with nothing written, and the caller can open the kit again read-only. Under `ReadOnlyKitAccess` no lock is taken. It then records the stamps (step 130); on an error there it releases the lock it took and passes the error through. `member this.close () : Result<unit, KitError>` releases the lock (the read-only view releases nothing). `member this.saveKitFile (kitFile : KitFile) : Result<KitContext, KitError>` sets `modified` to `this.clock.now ()`, writes the kit file through the context's proxy and answers the context holding it, so a read-only context answers `KitReadOnlyErr`. `modified` is when the app last wrote the kit file; the writes of the other kit files leave the `.kit-json` alone, so a git-managed kit does not churn it on every save. No other member is added: logic reads and writes the kit's other files through `context.proxy`. Tests. New `KitProxyContract` facts: `opening a kit read-write reads its kit file and takes the lock, and closing it releases the lock`: over an initialised folder, `openKit` of A with `ReadWriteKitAccess` answers a context holding the kit file and `ReadWriteKitAccess`, and `paths` holds `.oc/lock`; its `close ()` answers `Ok` and `paths` holds no `.oc/lock`. `a kit open elsewhere refuses a read-write open and allows a read-only one`: with A opened read-write, `openKit` of B with `ReadWriteKitAccess` answers `KitLockedErr` and `paths` is unchanged, and `openKit` of B with `ReadOnlyKitAccess` answers a context holding the same kit file and `ReadOnlyKitAccess`. `a read-only kit context writes nothing, not even the cache folder`: over a folder initialised with one experiment file written, with no lock taken, the read-only context's `proxy.initialiseKit`, `proxy.writeKitFile`, `proxy.writeKindFile`, `proxy.deleteKindFile` and `proxy.acquireLock` and its `saveKitFile` each answer `KitReadOnlyErr`; its `proxy.readKindFile` answers the experiment's text; its `close ()` answers `Ok`; and `paths` is exactly what it was before the open, with no `.oc`. `saving the kit file through the context stamps its modified time`: over a folder initialised with `newKitFile clock`, `openKit` of A with `ReadWriteKitAccess` and the same stepping clock, then `saveKitFile` of the opened kit file with `notes = KitNotes "Reviewed"`, answers a context whose kit file's `modified` is two seconds after its `created` (the clock answered the creation, then the lock, then the save) and whose `created` is unchanged, and B's `readKitFile` answers that kit file. `opening a folder that is not a kit writes nothing`: `openKit` of a fresh folder answers `NotAKitFolderErr` under both accesses, and `paths` stays empty. `every kit error renders its condition and its payload` (step 122) covers `KitReadOnlyErr`.

**Acceptance.** After `KitContext` lands, `KitContext.openKit` MUST take the lock only under `ReadWriteKitAccess`, and a read-only context MUST write nothing, not even `.oc`; constructor-unit-tests MUST pass, including `opening a kit read-write reads its kit file and takes the lock, and closing it releases the lock`, `a kit open elsewhere refuses a read-write open and allows a read-only one`, `a read-only kit context writes nothing, not even the cache folder`, `saving the kit file through the context stamps its modified time`, `opening a folder that is not a kit writes nothing` under `KitProxyTests` and `KitProxyFolderTests` and the widened `every kit error renders its condition and its payload`, the count rising by 10; and build, elevation and the eleven s15 invariants MUST pass.
